Hi everyone,

Here’s a summary of the key information Ella covered, including guidance on letter formatting, content expectations, common issues being seen in checks, and questions raised during the meeting.
The session was held to clarify common issues and trends being noticed in letters, and to make sure everyone is applying a consistent approach when preparing outcomes.

Please note, this is not the documentation Ella advised they would provide, that will come in a separate email correspondence at a later time.

The main focus of the meeting was relating to:
•	Letter formatting and layout
•	Addressing taxpayers and entities correctly
•	What to include in the “You told us” section
•	What to include in the “We also considered” section
•	Reasons for decision
•	Penalty details tables
•	Notes and supporting documentation
•	Feedback, letter checks and escalation
•	How to seek support or clarification


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Name and title formatting
Names in letters should include the taxpayer’s title.
This includes:
•	Mr.
•	Mrs.
•	Ms.
•	Dr.
•	Any other relevant title.
This has recently been clarified as the current required approach under the style guide.
If e-letters automatically inserts the title, no need to remove it.

Reference number
When entering the reference number in the letter, make sure you are using the receipt ID from the outbound activity.
Important points:
•	Do not use the inbound receipt ID or either Activity IDs.
•	If the number starts with 501, it is likely the incorrect number.

Address block requirements
Tax agent portal requests
If the request was received through the tax agent portal, the address block must include “care of” with the firm name.
Use the format:
•	C/- Firm Name
For example:
Ella Robjohns
C/- H&R Block
123 Fake Street  Faketown  0001

Suburb, state and postcode spacing
There should be double spacing between:
•	Locality
•	State
•	Postcode
Format:
•	Locality  State  Postcode
It was noted that this issue may have recently been fixed in e-letters, but staff should still check the spacing during final clean-up.
•	However this issue should be fixed as of todays updates.

Salutations
Individual taxpayers
If the taxpayer is an individual and their first name is known:
•	Use: Dear [First Name]
•	Sign off with: Yours sincerely

Non-individual entities
For non-individual entities, the standard approach is:
•	Use: To whom it may concern
•	Sign off with: Yours faithfully
This applies to most non-individual entities, including:
•	Partnerships
•	Trusts
•	Super funds
•	Other entity types

Companies and government agencies
Ella advised that the ATO Outbound Interactions Editing Guide allows for a specific contact name to be used in limited circumstances, such as companies or government agencies where the specific contact is known.

However, for consistency with the current letter template, the preferred general approach is:
•	Use “To whom it may concern” for non-individual entities.
•	Use “Dear [First Name]” for individuals.

If APS6 or TL feedback appears to conflict with support guidance, raise the specific activity ID so it can be reviewed in context and, if needed, clarified with leadership.

Need help section
In the “Need help” section, make sure you choose the most appropriate option and delete the others.
For most requests, the correct option will be the online services option, which directs the taxpayer to use online services and visit the relevant website.
Do not use the online services option if:
•	The taxpayer has an overseas address
•	The request was received by telephony
•	The request was received by white mail
In those cases, use the more appropriate alternative option in the template.


“You told us” section
This section should summarise and rephrase the taxpayer’s contentions and circumstances.
Include all key points the taxpayer raised, even if some points do not appear directly relevant to the final decision.
The purpose is to show that:
•	Their full request was reviewed
•	Their circumstances were considered
•	They feel heard

What to include
Include:
•	Their main reasons for requesting remission
•	Relevant personal or business circumstances
•	Any explanations they provided
•	Any key dates or events they mentioned
•	Circumstances such as hardship, illness, overseas travel, tax agent issues, business impacts, or other relevant factors

Avoid duplication
If the taxpayer repeats the same reason multiple times, only include it once.
For example:
•	If they mention hardship in multiple parts of the request, one clear dot point is enough.

Rephrasing guidance
When rephrasing, stay close to the taxpayer’s version of events.
Do:
•	Condense long or repetitive information
•	Use professional language
•	Keep the meaning the same
•	Present the information clearly
Do not:
•	Change the substance of what they said
•	Add your own interpretation
•	Overstate or minimise their claim
•	Turn their statement into a conclusion they did not make

“You told us” wording
You do not need to start every dot point with:
•	“You told us…”
•	“You advised us that…”
This is because each dot point is already read as sitting under the “You told us” heading.

“We also considered” section
Ella noted that this is usually the most complex part of the letter.
This section should include facts from ATO systems that influenced the decision.
These should be factual, relevant, and specific to the decision being made.

What to include in “We also considered”
This section may include:
•	Previous penalties or interest
•	Previous remission requests and outcomes
•	Lodgement history
•	Payment history
•	Contact with the ATO
•	Correspondence issued by the ATO
•	Whether the taxpayer was made aware of outstanding obligations
•	Relevant compliance history
•	Relevant facts from ATO records

FTL and GIC considerations
Make sure the facts relate to the type of request.

FTL letters
For failure to lodge matters, focus on lodgement-related facts, such as:
•	Lodgement history
•	Late lodgement patterns
•	Outstanding lodgements
•	Lodgement reminders
•	Prior FTL penalties
Avoid including unrelated payment facts unless they are directly relevant.

GIC letters
For GIC matters, focus on payment-related facts, such as:
•	Payment history
•	Payment arrangements
•	Previous GIC remission requests
•	Outstanding debt
•	Whether payments were made
•	Whether the taxpayer contacted the ATO about payment issues

Examples of “We also considered” wording
Examples discussed included:
•	You have a history of late lodgement dating back to your 2007 business activity statement.
•	You have previously requested remission of GIC, and this was refused.
•	We also considered your contact with the ATO on 12 March 2025, during which you were made aware of your outstanding obligations.
•	Your business continued to operate and earn income during the relevant period.
Any facts included in this section should also be reflected in the notes.

Considering income
Income should not be used in isolation to make a decision.
The correct approach is to consider income in context.
This may include:
•	Whether income reduced compared to previous years
•	Whether the business continued operating
•	Whether the taxpayer still had capacity to meet obligations
•	Whether the overall circumstances support the request
•	The taxpayer’s full compliance and payment history
Do not simply say that because a taxpayer earned income, they should have paid.
Income is only one part of the overall picture.

How far back to look
There is no fixed rule for how far back to review.
It depends on:
•	The period covered by the request
•	The type of request
•	The taxpayer’s circumstances
•	The relevance of earlier behaviour
General guidance:
•	For compliance history, it may be appropriate to look further back.
•	For contact and correspondence, focus more closely on the relevant period.
•	If the request covers several years, review the relevant years and a reasonable period before them.

Reasons for decision
Use standard paragraphs from EKM wherever possible.
Preferred approach:
•	Use the EKM standard paragraph as written where it fits.
•	Slightly tailor it if it does not quite fit the taxpayer’s circumstances.
•	Avoid writing your own reasons from scratch where possible.
•	Avoid using AI-generated reasons unless they are checked carefully and align with EKM guidance.
A useful guide is to aim for one to three reason paragraphs, depending on the case.
This is not a strict rule, but more than three can make the letter look too dense.

Spacing between paragraphs
Do not add extra blank lines between standard paragraphs unless the template includes them.
The template should be followed as closely as possible.

Penalty details table
Amounts in the penalty details table should be formatted consistently.
Use:
•	Commas for thousands
•	Two decimal places for all amounts
Examples:
•	$0.00
•	$1,650.00

Penalty table formatting
General guidance:
•	Keep the table formatting as close to the template as possible.
•	Do not make unnecessary alignment changes.
•	If changing alignment, keep it consistent.
•	The period does not need to fit on one line.
•	It is acceptable for the period to appear over two lines, as shown in the template.
•	Remove unnecessary blank spaces left behind after deleting template instructions.
•	Minor table formatting issues should not normally cause a letter to fail on their own.

Page spacing and blank pages
If the table causes an extra blank page, try small formatting adjustments.
Options discussed included:
•	Reducing unnecessary space near the top of the letter
•	Using delete rather than backspace in some cases
•	Moving content slightly where appropriate
•	Moving the table down if only the heading appears at the bottom of a page
Avoid:
•	Leaving large blank spaces between the reasons and the table
•	Moving a whole table in a way that leaves half a page blank unnecessarily
•	Making drastic formatting changes that affect the professional appearance of the letter
If a table is long, it is acceptable for it to continue over multiple pages.

Letter dates
Remember to update the letter date before issuing.
This may be raised as a reminder during checks, but it should still be part of final clean-up.

Template as the formatting guide
The template should be treated as the main formatting guide.
If unsure about spacing, dot points, table layout, headings, or general formatting, compare the letter back to the template.
This includes:
•	Whether there should be extra blank lines
•	What dot points should look like
•	How the table should be laid out
•	Where headings should appear
•	Whether spacing has been accidentally added or removed

File naming
There was discussion about whether to use the activity ID or receipt ID when saving files.
Current practical guidance:
•	Procedure may refer to activity ID.
•	Support has been using receipt ID because it is also reflected in the letter and is more meaningful to the taxpayer or tax agent.
•	Either activity ID or receipt ID is acceptable for now, provided the file name is professional and relevant.
•	A letter should not fail solely because of whether the file was saved using the activity ID or receipt ID.
If anyone is failed only because of file naming, raise it with support or your TL.

Feedback and letter checks
If feedback is unclear, inconsistent, or appears to be based on preference rather than procedure, do not just accept it without understanding it.
You can:
•	Ask the support officer who reviewed the letter
•	Raise it with your TL
•	Ask for the letter to be reviewed again
•	Ask for clarification about why the change is required
•	Provide examples where feedback appears inconsistent
Support is aware that some staff have received inconsistent feedback. These concerns have been discussed within support, and the aim is to provide more consistent guidance going forward.

Feedback does not always mean failure
Not all comments on a checked letter mean the letter has failed.
Some comments may be FYI feedback only.
If it is unclear whether a comment is critical or minor, ask the reviewing support officer or your TL.

Formatting feedback
Minor formatting feedback may be provided as an FYI.
Examples may include:
•	Extra blank lines
•	Date needing to be updated
•	Minor table spacing
•	Template clean-up
These should not generally cause a letter to fail by themselves.
If a letter appears to have failed only due to minor formatting, raise it with your TL or support for review.

Personal preference vs required changes
If feedback appears to be based on personal preference rather than procedure or style guidance, ask for clarification so the required change is understood.
Where possible, feedback should be linked back to:
•	Procedure
•	Style guide requirements
•	Template requirements
•	Risk to the ATO
•	Professional tone or readability
If the reason for a change is unclear, ask for the change to be explained.

Support process
For small questions, first try to use your peer group.
Suggested order:
1.	Ask someone nearby or a signed-off colleague.
2.	Search the support channel to see if the question has already been answered.
3.	Raise a support ticket if the answer is not available.
4.	Wait for the ticket response before seeking further support.
5.	If the answer is unclear, follow up with the support officer or raise a further ticket.
Before raising a ticket, search the support channel, as many common questions have already been answered there.
Once a support ticket has been raised, wait for the response before separately messaging support. If the response does not resolve the issue, follow up with the support officer or raise a further ticket.
Support tickets help leadership understand the volume and type of support being provided, so they are preferred for anything more than a very quick question.

Notes
Notes should be completed before the letter is checked.
This allows support to confirm that:
•	The decision-making is sound
•	The notes support the letter
•	The facts and findings match the “We also considered” section
•	The activity has been completed correctly, except for publishing or issuing the letter

Note content
The EKM note template should be used, but Ella noted that it can be limited.
Do not remove required parts of the template.
You can add extra sections where useful.
A suggested structure was:
•	Type of remission request
•	Whether remission was granted or refused
•	Who made the request
•	Amount requested
•	Reference to the attached remission form
•	Facts and findings
•	Decision outcome
•	Reasons for decision
•	Delegation statement
The facts and findings in the notes should reflect the same facts used in the letter.

Professional language in notes
Notes should be:
•	Factual
•	Objective
•	Professional
•	Clear
Taxpayers may request access to their records, so notes must be written appropriately.

Telephony requests
For telephony requests, the telephony form should be completed and attached to the activity.
Ella advised that for telephony requests:
•	Do not leave a long note instead of completing the form.
•	Include the relevant information in the telephony Excel sheet.
•	Attach the completed form to the activity.
•	Follow the procedure for telephony requests.

Questions raised and answers

Should non-individual entities always be addressed “To whom it may concern”?
Generally, yes.
For most non-individual entities, use “To whom it may concern” and “Yours faithfully”.
There are limited situations where a company or government agency may use a specific contact name, but the preferred standard approach for the current letters is to use “To whom it may concern” for non-individuals.

If a business portal request includes a person’s name, should we use that name?
It depends on the entity type and context.
If it is an individual taxpayer, use their first name.
If it is a non-individual entity, the safer standard approach is usually “To whom it may concern”.
If the request involves a company and the specific contact is known, there may be room to use the contact name, but this should be considered carefully and consistently with the style guide.

If the letter says “Dear [Name]”, what sign-off should be used?
Use “Yours sincerely”.

If the letter says “To whom it may concern”, what sign-off should be used?
Use “Yours faithfully”.

Should “You told us” be rewritten in our own interpretation?
No.
Rephrase and condense the taxpayer’s points, but stay close to what they actually said.
Do not change the content or meaning of their claims.

Should “You told us” include irrelevant circumstances?
Yes, if the taxpayer raised them.
Even if a point does not strongly affect the decision, include it if it was part of their request so they can see it was considered.

Should standard paragraphs be used exactly as they are?
Use EKM standard paragraphs as written wherever they fit.
Tailor them only where they do not quite fit the taxpayer’s circumstances.

How many reason paragraphs should be included?
There is no strict rule, but one to three is generally a good guide.
More than three can make the letter look dense.

Should notes be completed before or after the letter check?
Before the letter check.
The notes should be in place so support can review the decision-making and confirm the letter and notes align.

Can we add to the note template?
Yes.
Do not remove required parts of the EKM template, but you can add additional relevant sections or information.

Should formatting issues cause a letter to fail?
Minor formatting issues should not generally cause a letter to fail on their own.
If this appears to happen, raise it with your TL or support.

What should I do if I do not understand why a letter was rejected?
Ask for clarification.
Start with the support officer who reviewed the letter or raise it with your TL.
If the reason is still unclear, ask for the letter to be reviewed again or escalated.

What should I do if I receive conflicting feedback?
Raise the specific activity ID with your TL or support.
This allows the feedback to be reviewed in context and clarified with the relevant people.

Resources to be provided
Ella advised that a follow-up email will include relevant resources, including:
•	The ATO Outbound Interactions Editing Guide
•	Relevant style guide information
•	A blank FTL refusal letter with comments showing common mistakes
•	Potential examples or templates for notes, if available
•	Additional clarification for TLs, APS6s and support officers


Please remember to:
•	Use titles in names.
•	Use the outbound receipt ID in the letter reference.
•	Use the correct salutation and sign-off.
•	Keep “You told us” close to the taxpayer’s version of events.
•	Use “We also considered” for relevant ATO system facts.
•	Make sure notes and letters reflect each other.
•	Use EKM standard paragraphs where possible.
•	Keep formatting consistent with the template.
•	Update the letter date before issuing.
•	Treat the template as the main formatting guide.
•	Raise unclear, inconsistent or preference-based feedback rather than guessing.
•	Search the support channel before raising a new ticket.
•	Use the support ticket process where appropriate.




